\section{Introduction}

We begin with situations in which \emph{real Jordan algebras} are intimately
connected with real differentiable manifolds, especially the formally real
Jordan algebras with the positive cones.

\section{Riemannian Symmetric Spaces}

A \emph{Riemannian manifold} is a smooth $C^{\infty}$-manifold $M$ carrying a
Riemannian metric, a smoothly varying positive definite inner product
$\left\langle \left.  {}\right\vert \right\rangle _{p}$ on the tangent space
$T_{p}(M)$ to the manifold at each point $p$. For simplicity we will assume
that all our manifolds are connected. An isomorphism $f$ of Riemannian
manifolds is a diffeomorphism of smooth manifolds whose differential
$df\left(  p\right)  $ is isometric on each tangent space, i.e., preserves the
inner product
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left.  df\left(  p\right)  \left(  u\right)  \right\vert
df\left(  p\right)  \left(  v\right)  \right\rangle _{p}=\left\langle \left.
u\right\vert v\right\rangle _{p}.
\end{equation}
Recall that the differential $df\left(  p\right)  $ lives on the tangent space
$Tp(M)$ with values
\begin{equation}
df\left(  p\right)  \left(  v\right)  =\frac{\partial f\left(  p\right)
}{\partial v}=\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow0}\left\{  \frac{1}{t}\left(  f\left(
p+tv\right)  -f\left(  p\right)  \right)  \right\}
\end{equation}
representing the directional derivative of $f$ in the direction of the tangent
vector $v$ at the point $p$.

In such manifolds we can talk of \emph{geodesics (paths of \textquotedblleft
locally shortest length\textquotedblright)}, and at $p$ there is a unique
geodesic curve $\U{3b3} _{pv}(t)$ (defined for $t$ in a neighborhood of $0$)
passing through $p$ at $t=0$ with tangent vector $v$. At each point p there is
a neighborhood on which the geodesic symmetry $\U{3b3} _{pv}(t)\rightarrow
\U{3b3} _{pv}(-t)$ is an involutive local diffeomorphism [in general not an
isometry with respect to the Riemann metric] having $p$ as isolated fixed point.

The exponential map $\exp\left(  p\right)  $ maps a neighborhood of $0$ in
$T_{p}(M)$ down onto a neighborhood of $p$ in the manifold via $v\rightarrow
\U{3b3} _{pv}(1)$, so that the straight line segments through the origin in
the tangent space go into the local geodesics through $p$. In case $M$ is
complete (the geodesics $\U{3b3} _{pv}(t)$ are defined for all $t$),
$\exp\left(  p\right)  $ maps all of $T_{p}(M)$ down onto all of $M$.

A \emph{Riemannian symmetric space} is a \emph{Riemannian manifold }$M$ having
at each point $p$ a symmetry $S_{p}$ (an involutive global isometry of the
manifold having $p$ as isolated fixed point, equivalently having differential
$1$ at $p$). The existence of such symmetries is a very strong condition on
the manifold. For example, it forces $M$ to be \emph{complete and the group of
all isometries of }$M$\emph{ to be a real Lie group }$G$\emph{ acting
transitively on} $M$, in particular it forces the manifold to be \emph{real
analytic instead of merely smooth}. Then the symmetries $S_{p}$ are unique and
induce the geodesic symmetry about the point $p$ [$S_{p}$($\exp\left(
p(v)\right)  $) = $\exp\left(  p(v)\right)  $]. Note that by uniqueness any
isometry $g\in G$ conjugates the symmetry $S_{p}$ at a point $p$ to the
symmetry $gS_{p}g^{-1}$ at the point $g(p)$. Thus we can rephrase the
condition for symmetry for $M$ as

\begin{enumerate}
\item there is a symmetry $S_{p_{0}}$ at one point $p_{0}$,

\item isometries act transitively on $M$ .
\end{enumerate}

The subgroup $K$ of $G$ fixing a point $p$ is compact and $M$ \emph{is
isomorphic to} the coset $G/K$, which leads to the traditional Lie-theoretic
approach to symmetric spaces.

In 1969 Loos gave an algebraic formulation of \emph{symmetric spaces} which
clearly revealed a \emph{Jordan connection}: 

\begin{enumerate}
\item A symmetric space is a Hausdorff $C^{\infty}$ manifold with a
differentiable multiplication $x$\textperiodcentered$y$ whose left
multiplications $s_{x}(y)=x$\textperiodcentered$y$ 

\item satisfies the \emph{symmetric axioms} 

\begin{enumerate}
\item $s_{x}$ is an involutive map: $s_{x}^{2}$ $=I_{M}$, 

\item $s_{x}$ satisfies the Fundamental Formula: $s_{xy}=s_{x}s_{y}s_{x},$

\item $x$ is an isolated fixed point of $s_{x}$. Here $s_{x}$ represents the
symmetry at the point $x\in M$ . 
\end{enumerate}

\item If one fixes a base point $c\in M$ , the maps $Q_{x}:=s_{x}s_{c}$
satisfy the usual Fundamental Formula $Q_{Q_{xy}}=$ $Q_{x}Q_{y}Q_{x}$. Here
$Q_{c}=I_{M}$ and $j=s_{c}$ is \textquotedblleft inversion.\textquotedblright\ 

\item For example, in any Lie group the product $x\cdot y$ $:=xy^{-1}x$ gives
such a multiplication, and for $c=e$ the maps $Q,j$ take the form
$Q_{x}(y)=xyx,$ $j(x)=x^{-1}$ in terms of the group operations.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Self-Dual Cones}

\emph{Certain }symmetric spaces can be described more fruitfully using
\emph{Jordan algebras}. For instance, there is a $1$ $-1$ correspondence
between the \emph{self-dual open homogeneous cones} in $\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and
$n$-dimensional \emph{formally real Jordan algebras}, wherein the geometric
structure is intimately connected with the Jordan algebraic structure living
in the tangent space.

A subset $\mathcal{C}$ of a real vector space $V$ is convex if it is closed
under convex combinations
\begin{equation}
tx+(1-t)y\in\mathcal{C\quad}\forall x,y\in\mathcal{C}\text{ and }0\leq t\leq1,
\end{equation}
and therefore connected. A set is a cone if it is \emph{closed under positive
dilations}%
\begin{equation}
tx\in\mathcal{C}\forall x\in\mathcal{C}\text{ and }t>0.
\end{equation}
An \emph{open convex cone is regular if it contains no affine lines}.

The dual of a regular open convex cone $\mathcal{C}$ is the cone
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{C}^{\ast}=\left\{  \left.  l\in V^{\ast}\right\vert l\left(
v\right)  >0\right\}
\end{equation}
of functionals which are \emph{strictly positive }on the original cone. 

\begin{example}
For example, for $0<\U{3b8} <2\U{3c0} $ the wedge of angle $\U{3b8} $ given
by
\begin{equation}
W_{\U{3b8} }:=\{z\in\mathbb{C}|-\U{3b8} <\arg(z)<\U{3b8} \}
\end{equation}
is an open cone in $\mathbb{R}^{2}$ $\cong$ $\mathbb{C}$, which is convex
$\operatorname*{Iff}$ $\U{3b8} \leq\U{3c0} $ and is regular
$\operatorname*{Iff}$ $\U{3b8} <\U{3c0} .$
\end{example}

A positive-definite bilinear form $\U{3c3} $ on $V$ allows us to identify
$V^{\ast}$ with $V$ and $\mathcal{C}^{\ast}$ with the cone $\left\{  \left.
y\in V\right\vert \U{3c3} (y,\mathcal{C})>0\right\}  $ in $V$ and we say that
a cone is\emph{ self-dual with respect to }$\U{3c3} $ if under this
identification it coincides with its dual. For example, the dual of the above
wedge $W_{\U{3b8} }$ with respect to the usual inner product $\U{3c3} $ on
$\mathbb{R}^{2}$ is $W_{\U{3b8} }^{\ast}$ $=$ $W_{\pi-\U{3b8} }$, so
$W_{\U{3b8} }$ is self-dual with respect to $\U{3c3} $ only for $\U{3b8}
=\U{3c0} $.

\section{Formally Real Jordan Algebras}

The formally real Jordan algebras, $J,$ investigated by Jordan, von Neumann,
and Wigner are precisely the finite-dimensional real unital Jordan algebras
such that every element has a unique spectral decomposition
\begin{equation}
x=\U{3bb} _{1}e_{1}+\text{\textperiodcentered\textperiodcentered
\textperiodcentered}+\U{3bb} _{r}e_{r}%
\end{equation}
for a supplementary family of orthogonal idempotents
\begin{equation}
I=e_{1}+\text{\textperiodcentered\textperiodcentered\textperiodcentered}+e_{r}%
\end{equation}
and an increasing family%
\begin{equation}
\U{3bb} _{1}<\text{\textperiodcentered\textperiodcentered\textperiodcentered
}<\U{3bb} _{r}%
\end{equation}
of distinct real eigenvalues. This family, the spectrum of $x$, is uniquely
determined as
\begin{equation}
\operatorname*{Spec}(x)=\{\left.  \U{3bb} \in\mathbb{C}\right\vert \left(
\U{3bb} I-x\right)  ^{-1}\text{ }\nexists\text{ in the complexification
}J_{\mathbb{C}}\}.
\end{equation}
In analogy with Jordan canonical form for matrices, we can say that the
elements all have a diagonal Jordan form with real eigenvalues. 

The spectral decomposition leads to a functional calculus: Every real valued
function $f\left(  t\right)  $ defined on a subset $S\subseteq\mathbb{R}$
induces a map on those $x\in J$ whose spectrum lies wholly in $S$ via
\begin{equation}
f(x)=\sum_{k}f(\U{3bb} _{k})e_{k}.
\end{equation}
In particular, the invertible elements are those with
\begin{equation}
\operatorname*{Spec}(x)\subseteq\mathbb{R}\backslash\{0\},
\end{equation}
and $f(t)=1/t$ induces inversion
\begin{equation}
f(x)=x^{-1}=\sum_{k}\U{3bb} _{k}^{-1}e_{k}.
\end{equation}
[When $f$ is the discontinuous function $f$ is defined on all of
$S=\mathbb{R}$ by $f(t)=1/t$ for $t\neq0$ and $f(0)=0$, then $f(x)$ is the
generalized inverse or pseudo-inverse of any $x$.] 

If $S$ is open and $f(t$) is continuous, smooth, or given by a power series as
a function of $t$, then so is $f(x)$ as a function of $x$. It is clear that
the set of positive elements (those $x$ with positive spectrum
$\operatorname*{Spec}(x)\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}$) coincides with the set of
invertible square
\begin{equation}
x^{2}=\sum_{k}\U{3bb} _{k}^{2}e_{k}%
\end{equation}
and also the exponentials
\begin{equation}
\exp(x)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n!}x^{n}=\sum_{k}e_{k}^{\U{3bb} }e_{k},
\end{equation}
and can (with some effort) be described as the connected component of the
identity element in the set $J^{-1}$ of invertible elements. \emph{The set of
positive elements is called the positive cone }$C(J)$\emph{ of the formally
real Jordan algebra }$J$.

\begin{theorem}
Positive Cone Theorem. The positive cone  $\mathcal{C}(J)$ of an
$n$-dimensional formally real Jordan algebra $J$ is an open regular convex
cone in $J=\mathbb{R}^{n}$ that is self-dual with respect to the positive
definite bilinear trace form $\U{3c3} (x,y)=\operatorname{Tr}(x^{\dagger
}y)=(x,y)$. The linear operators $U_{x}$ for $x$ generate a group $G$ of
linear transformations acting transitively on $\mathcal{C}(J)$. Since
$\mathcal{C}(J)$ is a connected open subset of $J,$ it is naturally a smooth
manifold, and we may identify the tangent space $T_{p}(x)$ with $J$; taking
\begin{equation}
(x,y)=\U{3c3} (Up-1x,y)
\end{equation}
at each point gives a $G$-invariant Riemannian metric on . Thus the
\emph{positive cone }$\mathcal{C}(J)$ of a formally real Jordan algebra is in
a \emph{canonical way a homogeneous Riemannian manifold}.
\end{theorem}

The inversion map
\begin{equation}
j:x\rightarrow x^{-1}%
\end{equation}
induces a diffeomorphism of $J$ of period $2$ leaving invariant and having
there a unique fixed point $1,$ [the fixed points of the inversion map are
$\left\{  e,f\right\}  ,$ where  $e+f=1$ are supplementary orthogonal
idempotents and those with $f=0$ lie in the other connected components of
$J^{-1}$], and provides a symmetry of the Riemannian manifold at $p=1$,  here
the exponential map is the ordinary algebraic exponential
\begin{equation}
exp1(x)=exfromT1(M)=JtoCone(J),
\end{equation}
and negation
\begin{equation}
x\sim\rightarrow-x
\end{equation}
in the tangent space projects to inversion e e- = e on the manifold. The
Jordan product and U-operator arise from the inversion symmetry
\begin{equation}
s1=jviadjx=Ux-1
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
d2j1(u,v)=\partial u\partial vj1=Vu(v)=2uv.
\end{equation}
If we choose a basis xk for J, then the Christoffel symbols determining the
affine connection at 1 are just the multiplication constants for the algebra:
xi \textbullet\ xj = k \U{393}k xk. Any other point p in one(J) can be
considered the unit element in its own algebraic system; since J[p] has the
same invertible elements as J, and by choice p lies in the same connected
component as e, so J[p] has the same connected component of the identity:
Cone(J[p]) = Cone(J). Therefore the manifold has a symmetry at the point p
given by x\qquad x[-1,p], the exponential map is expp(x) = e[x,p], and the
Christoffel symbols are just the multiplication constants of J[p] : xi
\textbullet p xj = k \U{393}k [p]xk. Thus every point is an isolated fixed
point of a symmetry, given by inversion in a Jordan isotope, and the self-dual
positive cone Cone(J) becomes a Riemannian symmetric space.


